  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the quadratic function \(f(x) = x^2 − 9x + 20\) in factored form.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the quadratic function in factored form.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(f(x) = (x − 4)(x − 5)\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What are the solutions of the quadratic equation \((x − 4)(x − 5) = 0\)?</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the solutions of the quadratic equation.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\(x = 4\) and \(x = 5\)</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Graph the quadratic function \(f(x) = x^2− 9x + 20\). </item>
</list>
<para>Use the Desmos graphing tool or technology outside the course. </para>

<div class="os-raise-ib-desmos-gc" data-schema-version="1.0"/>

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>What are the \(x\)-intercepts of the graph?</para>
  
  </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter the \(x\)-intercepts.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>\((4, 0)\), \((5, 0)\) or 4 and 5</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Marcus states that the zeros of the function of \(f(x) = x^2 − 9x + 20\) are 4 and 5. Do you agree? Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer and your reasoning.</para>
  
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para>Yes, 4 and 5 are the input values that result in an output of 0. When the function \(f(x) = x^2 − 9x + 20\) is graphed, it crosses the \(x\)-axis at \(x=4\) and \(x=5\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
